## The find worth knowing about

**The intermittent suite failure (#586) is solved, and it was never the
application.** `test_sftp.py` stands up five fake SSH servers, each with a
freshly generated host key on an ephemeral port — and the OS recycles those
ports. When a later server landed on a port an earlier one had used,
ShellMate compared the new key with the one it had remembered and correctly
refused. The fixture was two different machines claiming to be one host.

That explains every property the failure had: about one run in three,
because a collision needs the port pool to have wrapped; never when the file
ran alone, because forty other files are what cycles it; and
order-dependent, which is what made it look like a race. Proved by
reproducing the exact message from the kept log and then not reproducing it
after forgetting the entry.

It was only findable because the first step of the run made the runner keep
a failing file's output. Before that, every occurrence arrived as one word.


